Definition of Terms
Below are the technical and operational definitions used in this study:
Akaike Information Criterion
A statistical criterion used for model selection that evaluates the goodness of fit of a model while penalizing excessive complexity.
This refers to the statistical measure that will be used in this study to determine the optimal number of hidden volatility states in the Hidden Markov Model by comparing competing models with different state configurations.
Baum-Welch Algorithm
A specialized Expectation-Maximization algorithm for training Hidden Markov Models by estimating hidden state probabilities and updating model parameters iteratively.
This refers to the algorithm that trains the Hidden Markov Model and learns the hidden volatility structure of the Bitcoin market from historical data.
Bayesian Information Criterion
A statistical model selection criterion that evaluates model fit while applying a stronger penalty for model complexity compared to AIC.
This refers to the statistical criterion used to prevent overfitting during model selection.
Close price
The final traded price of an asset within a specified trading interval.
This refers to the price recorded at the end of each trading interval and serves as the basis for calculating logarithmic returns.
Expectation-Maximization Algorithm
An iterative optimization algorithm that estimates parameters in statistical models involving hidden or incomplete data.
This refers to the optimization process that estimates the parameters of the Hidden Markov Model, including transition probabilities and state distributions based on observed Bitcoin market data.
Hidden Markov Model (HMM)
A statistical model used to predict sequences of data by modeling systems with hidden, unobservable states that influence observable outcomes.
This refers to a statistical model that will be used to identify hidden market conditions or volatility levels using observed price data.
Highest Price
The maximum traded price of an asset during a specific trading interval.
This refers to the highest price recorded within each 5-minute, 15-minute, or 30-minute period and will be used to measure short-term price fluctuations and contribute to volatility assessment.
High-Frequency Price Data
Tick-by-tick information in financial markets, recording every transaction price, quote, and order book update, often at sub-millisecond precision.
This refers to price data recorded at short time intervals (5-minute, 15-minute, and 30-minute).
Log-returns
The logarithmic transformation of price ratios between consecutive time periods used to measure asset returns continuously over time.
This refers to the transformed cryptocurrency price returns computed from consecutive closing prices and used as the primary input feature for volatility modeling.
Lowest Price
The minimum traded price of an asset during a specific trading interval.
This refers to the lowest price reached within each 5-minute, 15-minute, or 30-minute period and will be used to assess intraperiod price variability and the high-low price range.
Short-Term Trading
Trading strategies in stock market or futures market in which the time duration between entry and exit is within a range of few days to few weeks.
This refers to the trading activities conducted within short time intervals (5, 15 and 30 minutes), where decisions are made based on rapid price movements and changing market volatility.
Trading Volume
The total quantity of an asset traded within a specific time interval.
This refers to the amount traded during each 5-minute, 15-minute, or 30-minute interval and will be used  as an indicator of market activity and trading intensity.
Viterbri Algorithm
A dynamic programming algorithm used in Hidden Markov Models to find the most likely sequence of hidden states known as Viterbri path that explains a sequence of observed events.
This refers to the algorithm that identifies the most likely volatility regime sequence (low, medium, or high volatility) for each time interval in the Bitcoin market data.
Volatility
The quality or state of being volatile, such as a tendency to change quickly and unpredictably.
This refers to the magnitude of cryptocurrency price fluctuations observed within specific trading intervals.
Volatility Clustering
A financial phenomenon where high-volatility periods tend to cluster together, followed by more high volatility, while calm periods are followed by continued low volatility.
This refers to the pattern where large price changes are followed by large changes, and small changes are followed by small changes.
Volatility Regime
The market environment based on the intensity of price fluctuations, typically clustering into low, medium, or high volatility states.
This refers to a specific state of market volatility based on the degree of price fluctuations and identified using the Hidden Markov Model.
Open price
The first traded price of an asset within a specified trading interval.
This refers to the initial price recorded at the beginning of each 5-minute, 15-minute, or 30-minute interval used in the dataset.







