METHODOLOGY
Research design
This study employs a quantitative research design focused on cryptocurrency volatility analysis using a Hidden Markov Model (HMM). It examines volatility behavior across Bitcoin, Ethereum, Dogecoin, and Litecoin using high-frequency market data, with emphasis on comparative analysis of volatility patterns and model performance across different feature sets, time intervals, and quarterly periods. 
The Hidden Markov Model serves as the core machine learning and statistical model of the study. Instead of predicting exact prices, the model is designed to identify underlying volatility regimes by analyzing statistical patterns present in market data. This approach enables the identification of changing market risk and regime transitions over time.
The study utilizes one year of historical cryptocurrency market data from January 2025 to December 2025. High-frequency OHLCV (Open, High, Low, Close, Volume) data represented through 5-minute, 15-minute, and 30-minute intervals will be used to capture short-term market fluctuations at different temporal granularities. 
To further assess the robustness of the model, multi-timeframe analysis will be conducted using 5-minute, 15-minute, and 30-minute intervals.
The overall methodology consists of data collection, preprocessing, feature engineering, Quarterly Segmentation, Multi-Time Frame Analysis,  Hidden Markov Model training, and model evaluation.

                                                                                                                                                                   










