Data Collection
Historical cryptocurrency market data will be collected from Binance covering the period from January 2025 to December 2025. The study focuses on four major cryptocurrencies, namely Bitcoin, Ethereum, Dogecoin, and Litecoin. These cryptocurrencies were selected due to their high market activity, trading popularity, and differing market characteristics, making them suitable for comparative volatility analysis. 
The dataset will consist of Open, High, Low, Close (OHLC) prices and trading volume, providing a complete representation of price movement and market activity within each time interval. From this dataset, the primary variable used for modeling will be the logarithmic return computed from closing prices, defined as: 
rt​=ln(Pt/Pt−1​) where Ptt​ represents the closing price at time t, and Pt−1 represents the previous closing price.
Logarithmic returns are used as the core input for volatility modeling due to their statistical properties, including time additivity and suitability for capturing continuous price changes.
In addition to closing price returns, the study also incorporates high, low, and volume data as part of the feature set to capture price movement and market activity. The high–low range reflects the extent of price fluctuations within each interval, while volume provides information on trading intensity and market participation.
A one-year timeframe of five-minute interval data will be used to ensure a sufficient number of observations for reliable Hidden Markov Model estimation and volatility regime analysis.
Data Preprocessing and Feature Engineering
Although cryptocurrency exchange data is generally structured, minimal preprocessing is still required to ensure consistency and reliability. The dataset will be checked for missing values, duplicates, and irregular timestamps, and will be aligned chronologically. Any abnormal price values caused by exchange errors will be inspected and treated appropriately.
After cleaning, feature engineering will be performed to transform raw market data into meaningful inputs for the model. The dataset consists of OHLCV variables, namely open, high, low, close, and volume, which represent both price movement and trading activity within each interval.
From this dataset, several features will be derived to better capture volatility behavior. The primary feature is the logarithmic return, computed from consecutive closing prices, which measures the intensity of price changes and serves as the core input for volatility modeling. In addition, rolling volatility will be calculated using the standard deviation of log returns over a window of 10 to 20 periods, allowing the model to capture changes in market variability over time.
To further represent intraperiod price movement, the high–low range will be included to capture the extent of price fluctuations within each interval. Lastly, trading volume will be incorporated as a measure of market activity, helping distinguish between strong and weak price movements depending on the level of participation in the market.
These engineered features collectively form the input to the Hidden Markov Model, enabling a more comprehensive representation of market behavior for volatility regime classification.

Quarterly Segmentation 
After data preprocessing and feature engineering, the historical cryptocurrency dataset will be divided into four quarterly periods to examine changes in volatility behavior throughout the one-year observation period. The quarterly segmentation will be applied separately to each cryptocurrency and time interval configuration (5-minute, 15-minute, and 30-minute datasets).
