This segmentation approach enables the study to examine whether the effectiveness of the selected feature sets varies across different market periods within the year. Since cryptocurrency markets are highly dynamic and influenced by changing trading conditions, dividing the dataset into quarterly periods allows the study to analyze model performance under different market conditions and temporal variations in volatility behavior.
The quarterly segmentation also supports comparative analysis across cryptocurrencies, time intervals, and feature configurations. By training the Hidden Markov Model separately for each quarterly dataset, the study can compare the performance of the baseline feature set consisting of logarithmic returns and high-low price range against the extended feature set that additionally includes trading volume and rolling volatility using evaluation metrics such as log-likelihood, Akaike Information Criterion (AIC), and Bayesian Information Criterion (BIC).
Multi-Timeframe Analysis
To evaluate how volatility behavior changes across different time resolutions, the study applies multi-timeframe analysis using 5-minute, 15-minute, and 30-minute intervals. The Hidden Markov Model is applied separately to each timeframe to identify volatility regimes.
The resulting regime classifications are then compared to determine whether volatility patterns remain consistent or vary depending on the time interval. This allows the study to assess the stability and reliability of the model across different market granularities.
Hidden Markov Model Training
The Hidden Markov Model (HMM) serves as the core machine learning component of the study. It assumes that observed market data (log returns and related features) are generated from a finite number of hidden states, each representing a distinct volatility regime. These states evolve over time following a Markov process, where the current state depends only on the previous state.
To determine the optimal number of volatility regimes, multiple models with different numbers of states will be evaluated using Akaike Information Criterion (AIC) and Bayesian Information Criterion (BIC). Based on financial theory and prior literature, the expected optimal solution is three states: low, medium, and high volatility.
Model parameters, including transition probabilities, initial state probabilities, and state-dependent distributions, will be estimated using the Baum–Welch algorithm, which is an Expectation–Maximization (EM) method. The EM procedure iteratively refines the model through two steps: the Expectation step, where the probabilities of hidden states are estimated based on the current parameters, and the Maximization step, where the parameters are updated to better fit the observed data.
This iterative process continues until convergence, allowing the model to stabilize its learned volatility structure. After training, the Viterbi algorithm will be used to determine the most probable sequence of hidden states, assigning each time point to a specific volatility regime.
This allows the model to perform continuous volatility regime classification suitable for real-time market analysis.
Model Evaluation and Validation
The performance of the Hidden Markov Model will be evaluated using log-likelihood, Akaike Information Criterion (AIC), and Bayesian Information Criterion (BIC). Log-likelihood will measure how well the model fits the observed cryptocurrency data, while AIC and BIC will be used to assess model quality by balancing goodness of fit and model complexity, with lower values indicating better performance.
The evaluation focuses on comparative analysis across multiple dimensions of the study, specifically across cryptocurrencies, quarterly periods, time intervals, and feature configurations. This includes comparing results among Bitcoin, Ethereum, Dogecoin, and Litecoin, as well as across the 5-minute, 15-minute, and 30-minute intervals, and the two feature sets used in the model.
In addition, comparative analysis will be conducted across quarterly segments (Q1 to Q4) to determine whether model performance and volatility representation vary across different market periods. This multi-dimensional evaluation framework allows the study to assess whether the inclusion of trading volume and rolling volatility improves the model’s ability to capture cryptocurrency volatility behavior under different market conditions.





